## Sold / delete

Follow the same triggers as Marktplaats (see `marktplaats-listings.mdc`), applied to both listings together:

- **Reserved (sold, money not in yet):** update the shop first (`reserved: true`, `soldAt`, the sale `price`; both URLs stay), set the Marktplaats ad to **Gereserveerd**, and then the Vinted listing: sold **on Vinted** → nothing, Vinted marks it sold itself and pays out once the buyer confirms; sold **elsewhere** → mark the listing reserved on Vinted. Neither listing is deleted until the money is in. See **Reserved** in `marktplaats-listings.mdc`.
- **Sold for €X:** update the shop first (per that rule), then delete the Marktplaats ad — as **Verkocht via Marktplaats** only if the sale was there, **Niet verkocht via Marktplaats** when it sold on Vinted — then delete the Vinted listing too, unless Vinted already closed it because the sale was there.
- **Delete without a sale:** set `concept: true`, remove both `marktplaatsUrl` and `vintedUrl`, do not set `sold`.

Vinted's delete/sold confirmation is an in-page choice, not a native `alert`/`confirm` — do not call `dialog.accept()`; click through whatever Vinted shows.

## Relisting

Vinted has no free bump, so a listing that has slid down the catalogue only comes back up as a new listing. The local admin has a **Vinted relist** tab (`/admin/vinted-relist`, dev server only) for this: it reads the seller's wardrobe with age, views and likes per listing, and each **Relist** button deletes that listing and uploads a copy — same title, description, category, brand, condition, price and package size — with the photos taken fresh from the product's originals (the branded ad in `public/ads/<cert>.jpeg`, then the slab front and back from `seed/media/`) rather than from the old listing, because Vinted re-encodes every upload and copied photos degrade with each relist; only a listing that matches no product gets its old photos copied. After publishing, Vinted lands on the seller's wardrobe (`/member/<id>?promo_shown=true`) rather than on the new listing's page, so the tool takes leaving the upload form as the signal that the listing is up and finds the copy in the wardrobe as the newest open listing with the old title and a higher id — it must not wait for an `/items/<id>` URL, which never comes and used to cost 90 seconds per relist. It then writes the new URL to the product's `vintedUrl`. It drives the dev server's own Chrome window (the `.cache/cardmarket-chrome` profile). When Vinted's session there has run out, the relist logs the window in itself with `VINTED_USERNAME` and `VINTED_PASSWORD` from `.env` — cookie banner answered with only the essential cookies first, then Vinted's email login page — once for the whole batch, and tabs that landed on their listing page before the login land again, since a logged-out listing page has no **Verwijderen**; a login Vinted turns down is reported with Vinted's reason and not typed in again for 30 minutes or until `.env` changes, and a code Vinted asks for is left in the tab for Timo. That is the dev server typing the shop's own login from `.env`; the rule above for Playwright MCP stands. A relist that fails after the delete is kept as a snapshot under `.cache/vinted-relist/` and shows a **Retry upload** button. Use the tab rather than deleting and re-posting by hand. If a card was re-posted by hand anyway (after a rate-limit block, say), the next read of the tab notices the card's listing is gone while a newer, unclaimed listing with its title is up, and points the product at that listing — as long as the pairing is unambiguous. A `sold` or `reserved` card is not on the tab — it is gone, so there is nothing to bump, and a listing Vinted closed itself over a sale there drops off as well — and the relist API refuses it (409) even from a tab opened before the sale.

**Relist all**, in the screen's header, queues every live listing that is not busy already, from the top of the list — the oldest — down to the youngest; while relists wait it turns into **Stop**, which lets the waiting ones go (the one under way finishes, since a relist cannot be called back halfway). A failed relist stops the ones still waiting: a failure after the delete (a changed upload form, say) would repeat on every listing after it and take the whole wardrobe off Vinted. Only a 409 — a card that sold meanwhile, or a listing another tab is relisting — is gone past.

Relists run up to `RELIST_TABS` at a time (two, in `app/services/vinted-relist.ts`), each in a Chrome tab of its own (`vite/tab-pool.ts`). The dev server keeps the rest in its one queue (`vite/relist-batch.ts` over `app/admin/relist-queue.ts`) and starts the next only once a slot is free, in the order asked for, so a batch goes on to its last listing with no admin open — a locked phone included — and every admin sees where it got to. A relist tab is kept for a minute once its relist is done (`RELIST_TAB_LINGER_MS`) and then closes, unless a relist is waiting or arrives within that minute, which takes the tab over — the next relist is seldom in the queue the moment one finishes, because the admin sends it only once the last one has answered, and it reaches the tabs only after the settle with production below; a relist that failed leaves its tab as it is — the login page, a bot check, the form that did not go through — until the window closes. Only the one tab that reads the wardrobe stays between reads. The screen labels each row **Queued…**, **Relisting…** or **Relisted** and reads the list again only once the whole batch is done, so it does not re-sort under the buttons; a listing whose relist is still under way is refused (409) if pressed again. What reaches Vinted is kept to the pace of one person: relists start four seconds apart; the tabs share one session check and every wardrobe read (the wardrobe a relist just read answers the screen's next look); a fresh tab lands straight on the listing page it is about to delete rather than on the homepage first; a "not logged in" found by one tab fails the rest of the batch without another request; and a rate limit hit in any tab starts the cool-down, at which the other tabs stop at their next step with their state written down. The state file is written through atomic read-change-write updates, never held across a wait — two relists must not lose each other's pending entry. Before each relist the dev server settles the local database with production — about one remote round trip per card, which also catches a card that sold elsewhere during a long batch; settles asked for while one is already queued share it. Do not raise `RELIST_TABS` casually: Vinted rate-limits by computer and a block takes the shop's own Vinted access down with it. `test/vinted-relist-flow.test.ts` drives the whole relist against a stand-in Vinted in headless Chrome; add to it rather than trying anything against the real site.

## Price changes

Vinted price always matches the current Marktplaats price exactly. When the Marktplaats Vraagprijs changes on a card with a `vintedUrl`, edit that Vinted listing's price to match — see **cms-inventory.mdc** for the sync-then-marketplace order of operations.
